#VRML V2.0 utf8
# CTR: the force field, made solid.
#
# The original Jail enforced its cell boundary in `DEF CheckMe Script` -- a vrmlscript that
# read `Browser.viewpointPosition` every frame and wrote `Browser.boundViewpoint.position`
# back when you crossed the line. Those are Blaxxun browser extensions. X_ITE 16.2.0 has
# neither, so the rule the authors wrote has not been enforced for anyone since the port,
# which is why visitors can stand in the cells today.
#
# This restores the same rule with something every VRML97 browser implements: collision.
# The box matches the force field polygon in jail.wrl -- `DEF forcefield`, a plane at
# z = 1.25 spanning x -3.25..3.25 and y 0..4 once its -90 degree X rotation is applied --
# so the thing that looks impassable finally is. It is a Collision PROXY and not a child,
# because a proxy is used for collision and never drawn: nothing about the Jail looks any
# different, and no historical geometry is touched. (A transparent Shape would not work --
# X_ITE culls fully transparent geometry before collision, so it would block nothing.)
#
# The same barrier serves both directions. In the visitor world it keeps visitors out of
# the cells; in the prisoner world it keeps inmates in. Only the staff world omits it, and
# only the server decides who is served that file -- see JailService.applyWorldForMember.
Collision {
  collide TRUE
  proxy Transform {
    translation 0 2 1.25
    children Shape {
      geometry Box {
        size 6.5 4 0.3
      }
    }
  }
  children []
}
